FILES AND MECHANISMS

1. MCP environment
   src/mcp.rs::scrub_bboxy_env removes every inherited or explicitly configured
   BBOXY_* name, including non-UTF8 names via their encoded prefix. Ordinary
   environment entries remain. Untrusted BBOXY_* configured entries are not
   resolved or executed. The existing reserved-capability alias check remains
   for non-launcher environments.
   The baseline already removed BBOXY_CAPABILITY specifically; it still leaked
   SEAT_ID, SOCK, ROLE, GENERATION and the custom prefix marker. The new rule
   covers the full namespace, including the explicit-env override path.

   Origin is private, immutable slot configuration assigned by select_sources;
   it is not a ServerConfig serde field. A profile/ACP server named bboxy or
   carrying launcher:true does not gain inheritance. Only servers supplied by
   the trusted host's launcher map retain BBOXY_*.
   Launcher values and the inherited capability are registered with the
   redactor before spawn. MCP calls still pass policy.decide; trust_hints is
   independent of launcher provenance.

2. Startup flags
   src/frontends/headless.rs accepts --append-system-prompt <text> and
   --mcp-config <path>; unknown options and missing values remain errors.
   The launcher file has the documented JSON mcpServers shape. Read/parse
   failures become durable Warn Notices and do not fail the turn.
   The file read/parse runs through the bounded blocking API.

   engine::LaunchOptions is separate from the existing Config struct and
   profile/ACP data. Host::with_launch carries it through create and open;
   the CLI also passes that host into ACP through serve_with_host.
   SessionActor appends the supplied text after the base system text in the
   existing system message. Therefore it is inside the stable prefix.
   The launcher map wins over ACP, ACP over profile. select_sources emits
   one final collision Notice per name. Tool names keep mcp__bboxy__<tool>.

3. Model classification
   Origin and launcher data are fixed before a Slot starts and remain fixed
   across its restarts. Selection, environment construction, file parsing
   and prompt concatenation are input transformations, not new
   HarnessMcpServer lifecycle transitions. verification/coverage.toml names
   these as implementation-only coverage and points to the actual unit and
   process witnesses. No environment property is falsely attributed to the
   existing lifecycle model.
   The mandatory native/TLC corpus still runs in full on the changed sources.

WITNESSES

- tests/support/seat_launch.rs, included by tests/seat_process.rs:
  profile and ACP servers each observe no BBOXY_* names; a launcher observes
  the synthetic seat id and capability. A profile server in that same session
  remains scrubbed. The lower-priority profile bboxy server is never spawned,
  and its collision yields exactly one Notice.
- With both flags, an actual seat process sends its first HTTP/SSE mock request
  with the supplied context in the system message and mcp__bboxy__read in its
  tools. Both server readiness events are observed before that first turn.
- engine's Recording provider also checks the first actual scripted
  ModelRequest.messages[..stable_prefix] directly for the appended text.
- Missing and unreadable launcher paths both print the Notice and reach
  EndTurn. Unknown options and missing flag arguments exit 1.
- Server JSON cannot forge launcher provenance; source selection is unit-tested.
- Every process-test parent clears its environment and supplies throwaway
  values. The fixture records names and boolean comparisons only, never
  capability values. No real key/capability value is printed or committed.
  check_transcript holds over the resulting durable session logs.

